# The trace indent a CALL ON handler is given when it is delivered at a plain
# DO's own two clause boundaries, pinned on the tree-walker's own bytes.
#
# A plain `DO` has a header clause and an `END` clause, and each settles the
# block indent a handler delivered there inherits. The two settle different
# values: the header clause has opened the block, so it settles a level in from
# the `DO`'s own indent; the `END` clause has closed it again, so it settles the
# `DO`'s own. `Interp::settle_block_indent` is what each calls, and its `open`
# argument is the whole of the difference -- a level being two columns.
#
# **The settled indent is not the column a handler's clauses echo at.** A
# handler activation echoes a level in from the block indent it inherits --
# visible in every row below -- so each row's handler sits two columns deeper
# than the value its boundary settled. Each row's own note gives the columns to
# check it against, each of them a count of the spaces between `*-*` and the
# clause text.
#
# **What each row is for.** The two top-level rows separate the arguments: each
# is built so that only its own boundary delivers a handler, and measured,
# flipping either `open` argument in `run_loop_with_header`'s `LoopKind::Simple`
# arm moves that argument's own row and leaves the other byte-identical, on both
# engines. The nested row separates `do_indent` from the constant it happens to
# equal at top level: its `DO` sits inside another block, so `do_indent` is 2
# rather than 0, and a site that ignored `do_indent` and passed a literal `0`
# would match both top-level rows. It delivers a handler at both boundaries, so
# it is not selective between the two arguments; that is what the rows above it
# are for.
#
# **The oracle differential cannot see any of this**, which is why the witness
# is here: `tests/support/mod.rs` normalises the width of a trace line's indent,
# and measured, `corpus_differential` in STRICT passes under either `open` flip
# and under a literal `0` at either site.
#
# What these rows do not pin: which argument carries the level. Only the settled
# indent reaches the handler, so `settle_block_indent(false, do_indent + 2)` at
# the header would produce these same bytes. Nor do they tell `do_indent` from
# the `end_indent` computed for the `END`'s own echo -- measured, passing that
# instead at the `END` site leaves every row below byte-identical. No row
# contains a repeating loop, so none says anything about the block indent a
# repeating `DO` settles.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE. Regenerate a
# row by running its program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 --
# `both_engines_agree_on_every_case_file` refuses to run under REWRITE for that
# reason. Capture the three descriptors separately, never merged:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null ) >out 2>err
#
# and write the block as `rc> <exit status>`, then every line of `out` tagged
# `out>`, then every line of `err` tagged `err>`.

# Delivered at the DO's header clause. `zn = ra()` queues zx, its own boundary
# runs `h`, and `h` requeues zy -- which the next boundary owes, and the next
# boundary is the `DO`'s own. The block is open there, so that boundary settles
# 2 and `g:` echoes at column 4, ahead of the body's `say` at column 2.
program
trace r
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
zn = ra()
do
say 'body'
end
say 'after' zn
exit 0
ra:
raise user zx return 1
h:
say 'h ran' sigl
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'g ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h ran 4
out> g ran 5
out> body
out> after 1
err>      2 *-* call on user zx name h
err>      3 *-* call on user zy name g
err>      4 *-* zn = ra()
err>     10 *-*   ra:
err>     11 *-*   raise user zx return 1
err>        >K>     "RESULT" => "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>     12 *-*   h:
err>     13 *-*   say 'h ran' sigl
err>        >>>     "h ran 4"
err>     14 *-*   raise user zy return 1
err>        >K>     "RESULT" => "1"
err>      5 *-* do
err>     15 *-*     g:
err>     16 *-*     say 'g ran' sigl
err>        >>>       "g ran 5"
err>     17 *-*     return
err>      6 *-*   say 'body'
err>        >>>     "body"
err>      7 *-* end
err>      8 *-* say 'after' zn
err>        >>>   "after 1"
err>      9 *-* exit 0
err>        >>>   "0"

# Delivered at the END clause. Nothing is queued before the `DO`, so its header
# boundary has nothing to deliver; the body clause queues zx, runs `h` at its
# own boundary, and `h`'s requeue of zy is owed to the `END`. The block is
# closed by then, so that boundary settles 0 -- the `DO`'s own -- and `g:`
# echoes at column 2, the body clause's own column.
program
trace r
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
do
zn = ra()
end
say 'after' zn
exit 0
ra:
raise user zx return 1
h:
say 'h ran' sigl
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'g ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h ran 5
out> g ran 6
out> after 1
err>      2 *-* call on user zx name h
err>      3 *-* call on user zy name g
err>      4 *-* do
err>      5 *-*   zn = ra()
err>      9 *-*     ra:
err>     10 *-*     raise user zx return 1
err>        >K>       "RESULT" => "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>     11 *-*     h:
err>     12 *-*     say 'h ran' sigl
err>        >>>       "h ran 5"
err>     13 *-*     raise user zy return 1
err>        >K>       "RESULT" => "1"
err>      6 *-* end
err>     14 *-*   g:
err>     15 *-*   say 'g ran' sigl
err>        >>>     "g ran 6"
err>     16 *-*   return
err>      7 *-* say 'after' zn
err>        >>>   "after 1"
err>      8 *-* exit 0
err>        >>>   "0"

# The same two boundaries with a non-zero `do_indent`. The inner `DO` sits in an
# outer block, so it echoes at column 2 and both of its boundaries settle from 2
# rather than 0: the header settles 4 and `g:` echoes at column 6, the `END`
# settles 2 and `m:` echoes at column 4. Measured, replacing `do_indent` with a
# literal `0` at either site moves that site's own handler two columns out --
# `g:` to 4, or `m:` to 2 -- and leaves the two rows above byte-identical.
#
# Two independent chains reach the two boundaries. zx is queued before the inner
# `DO` and `h`'s requeue of zy lands at its header; zw is queued by the inner
# body clause and `k`'s requeue of zv lands at the inner `END`. The outer block's
# own two boundaries deliver nothing.
program
trace r
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
call on user zw name k
call on user zv name m
do
zn = ra()
do
zp = rb()
end
end
say 'after' zn zp
exit 0
ra:
raise user zx return 1
rb:
raise user zw return 1
h:
say 'h ran' sigl
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'g ran' sigl
return
k:
say 'k ran' sigl
raise user zv return 1
m:
say 'm ran' sigl
return
